Comprehensive Validation: The Pattern
The Problem You've Experienced:
Remember your volumes project? You said:

"Minor changes in worksheet structure would break downstream processes which needed to be fixed manually."

What was happening:

Data arrives
You discover it's wrong AFTER it's already in the system
You manually fix it
Repeat monthly

This is reactive error handling. You're the quality control.

Comprehensive Validation flips this:

Data arrives
System AUTOMATICALLY checks rules
System FLAGS issues BEFORE they cause problems
Analyst reviews flagged items (not everything)
Only clean data flows to reports

This is proactive error prevention. The system is the quality control.

The Pattern: Separate Validation Table
Look at Data_Quality_Flags in the data model:
Data_Quality_Flags
├── FlagID (Primary Key)
├── SubmissionID (Which submission has an issue)
├── FlagType (What kind of issue)
├── Severity (How serious)
├── Description (Human-readable explanation)
├── ThresholdValue (What we expected)
├── ActualValue (What we got)
├── FlaggedDate (When detected)
├── Status (Open/Dismissed/Resolved)
├── ResolvedBy (Who handled it)
├── ResolvedDate (When handled)
└── ResolutionNotes (How it was handled)
Key principle: Validation issues are stored SEPARATELY from the data itself.
Why?

One submission can have multiple issues
Issues have their own lifecycle (flagged → reviewed → resolved)
Can analyze validation trends over time


