SharePoint Reality Check (The Constraints)
Before we design schemas, you need to understand SharePoint's limitations. This is where the beautiful normalized data model meets real-world constraints.
Constraint 1: List View Threshold (5,000 Items)
The Rule:

SharePoint lists can HOLD millions of items
But queries can only return 5,000 items at once (without indexing)
Exceed this = "List view threshold exceeded" error

Impact on our design:
Volume_Submissions will grow:
- 45 countries × 10 test types × 12 months = 5,400 records per year
- After 1 year, you hit the threshold
- After 2 years, you're at 10,800 records
Solution: Indexed Columns

SharePoint allows 20 indexed columns per list
Index = fast lookup even with 100,000+ items
We'll index: SubmissionMonth, CountryID, Status, IsDeleted


Constraint 2: Lookup Column Limit (12 Lookups)
The Rule:

Lookup columns connect lists (foreign keys)
Maximum 12 lookup columns per list
Lookups can be slow with large lists

Impact on our design:
Volume_Submissions has:
- CountryID (lookup to Countries)
- TestTypeID (lookup to TestTypes)
- SubmittedBy (lookup to Users)
- ValidatedBy (lookup to Users)
- LastModifiedBy (lookup to Users)

Total: 5 lookups - we're safe
But if we add more relationships, we could hit the limit.

Constraint 3: Calculated Columns (Limited Complexity)
The Rule:

Calculated columns can do math/logic
But limited functions (no web service calls)
Calculate at item creation, not query time

Impact on our design:
DataQualityScore could be calculated column:
= IF([IsDeleted]=TRUE, 0, IF([Status]="Approved", 100, 70))

But complex scores need Power Automate

Constraint 4: Choice Columns (255 Choices Max)
The Rule:

Choice columns = dropdown lists
Max 255 choices
Good for Status, FlagType, etc.

Impact on our design:
Status column (Choice):
- Draft
- Submitted  
- Pending_Review
- Validated
- Flagged
- Approved
- Rejected

7 choices - perfect for Choice column

Constraint 5: Person Columns (Azure AD Integration)
The Rule:

Person/Group columns link to Azure AD automatically
Don't need separate Users table for basic scenarios
Can lookup user properties (email, name, department)

Impact on our design:
Instead of:
  SubmittedBy (lookup to Users table)

Use:
  SubmittedBy (Person column - built-in Azure AD)
This simplifies our model for MVP.

SharePoint Schema Design Strategy
Principle 1: Start Normalized, Denormalize for Performance
Our approach:

Keep core entities separate (Countries, TestTypes, Submissions)
Use lookups where appropriate
Accept some denormalization for usability


Principle 2: Index Early, Index Often
Index these columns:

Primary filters (Month, Country, Status)
Foreign keys (CountryID, TestTypeID)
Date columns (SubmittedDate, ValidatedDate)
IsDeleted flag


Principle 3: Use SharePoint Features When Possible
Built-in features:

Person columns (not custom Users table)
Created/Modified metadata (automatic)
Version history (audit trail for free)
Document libraries (for file storage)


