Part 2: Countries with Multiple Timezones (USA Problem)
You're absolutely right. This is a real issue.
The Problem
USA has 4 mainland timezones:

Eastern: UTC-5 (NYC, Atlanta)
Central: UTC-6 (Chicago, Dallas)
Mountain: UTC-7 (Denver, Phoenix)
Pacific: UTC-8 (LA, Seattle)

If USA is one country in our system:
tbl_Countries:
Title: "United States"
TimezoneOffset: ??? (which one?)
We can't represent multiple timezones with one offset.

The Solution: It Depends on Granularity
Question: Who is submitting volumes?
Our design assumption:
One coordinator per country submits consolidated volumes for that country
Example:

USA coordinator: Based in NYC (Eastern Time)
They collect volumes from all US training centers
They submit ONE consolidated submission for "USA"

In this case:

TimezoneOffset = -5 (Eastern)
Represents the coordinator's timezone, not every training center

This works because:

We don't track individual training centers
We only track country-level submissions
Coordinator's timezone determines "when was this submitted relative to deadline"


When This Breaks Down
If we tracked city-level submissions:
USA - New York: 1000 volumes (submitted 9 AM EST)
USA - Los Angeles: 850 volumes (submitted 9 AM PST = 12 PM EST)
USA - Chicago: 920 volumes (submitted 9 AM CST = 10 AM EST)
Now we'd need:

City table
City timezone offset
Country → City → Submissions (more complex hierarchy)

But this isn't our use case.

Our Actual Use Case (Simplified)
What we're really tracking:
Scenario 1: Single-timezone countries (Most countries)
Korea: UTC+9 (entire country)
Japan: UTC+9 (entire country)
France: UTC+1 (entire country)
Scenario 2: Multi-timezone countries (Edge case)
USA: Use coordinator's timezone (Eastern = UTC-5)
  - OR -
USA: Use HQ timezone (wherever corporate office is)
Scenario 3: Regional submission (Your France example)
France coordinator submits for ALL of Europe
European countries span UTC+0 to UTC+3

Solution: Use France's timezone (UTC+1) since that's where coordinator is

The Pragmatic Answer
For this project:
TimezoneOffset = Coordinator's timezone OR HQ office timezone
Not: Every possible timezone in the country
Why this works:

Submission deadline is relative to coordinator

"3rd business day" means coordinator's 3rd business day
If USA coordinator is in NYC, deadline is 5 PM EST on 3rd business day


We're not tracking individual training centers

Volumes are aggregated at country level
We don't care that LA center submitted at 9 AM PST vs NYC submitted at 9 AM EST


Timezone is for display/reporting, not critical business logic

Nice to know: "Korea submits at 10 AM their time = 9 PM our time"
Not critical: We don't charge late fees based on timezone precision




