2. Validation - Actually, We DO Need Some
You said "I don't think so" - but let me show you where validation prevents problems:
Validation Rules We Should Add
CountryCode:
Validation formula: 
  AND(LEN([CountryCode])=3, [CountryCode]=[CountryCode].UPPER())

Error message: 
  "Country code must be exactly 3 uppercase letters (ISO 3166-1 alpha-3 standard)"

Examples:
  Valid: "KOR", "JPN", "FRA"
  Invalid: "Korea" (too long), "kr" (lowercase), "K1" (contains number)
Why this matters:

Prevents typos that break lookups
Ensures consistency with international standards
Makes TM1 export reliable


TimezoneOffset:
Validation formula:
  AND([TimezoneOffset]>=-12, [TimezoneOffset]<=14)

Error message:
  "Timezone offset must be between -12 and +14"

Examples:
  Valid: +9, -5, +5.5 (India)
  Invalid: +25 (typo), -15 (doesn't exist)
Why this matters:

Prevents impossible timezones
Catches data entry errors
Makes time calculations reliable


SubmissionDeadlineDay:
Validation formula:
  AND([SubmissionDeadlineDay]>=1, [SubmissionDeadlineDay]<=28)

Error message:
  "Deadline day must be between 1 and 28 (to accommodate February)"

Examples:
  Valid: 3, 5, 15
  Invalid: 0, 31 (some months don't have 31 days)
Why this matters:

February only has 28/29 days
Prevents deadlines that don't exist in some months
Ensures deadline logic works year-round


